{"specification":{"scope":"Original WBXBAR public bus boundary, revision 2e8d3bc2d26ddc33d1881022a2a2b9d3f0c16b9b. Two masters, two slaves. No internal state or submodule observation.","authority":[{"source":"https://cdn.opencores.org/downloads/wbspec_b4.pdf","sha256":"d712d61fe7f363b671e6b821b7b0259f43ec0b4ce8f3b2072172b2878f347a5a","representation":"Maintainer paraphrase checked against PDF pages 31–38; not a new protocol specification.","requirements":[{"location":"3.1.1, rules 3.00–3.20, pp31–32","summary":"Reset takes effect on rising clock edges. Hold reset for at least one complete cycle. Master CYC and STB are inactive during reset; other master payload values are unspecified."},{"location":"3.1.2, rules 3.25/3.30, p33","summary":"CYC qualifies bus activity and spans a transfer cycle. Without CYC, slave request signals are inactive. System reset remains effective."},{"location":"3.1.3.2, rules 3.57–3.59, pp36–37; observation 3.10, p32","summary":"Pipelined acceptance requires CYC and STB high and STALL low. WE distinguishes writes from reads. New requests need not wait for earlier acknowledgments. ACK can occur while STB is low. Successful transactions retire with acknowledgments; read data is meaningful with ACK."},{"location":"permission 3.20 / rule 3.45, p35; rule 3.60, p37","summary":"ERR reports failure; the base protocol leaves the master reaction unspecified. ACK, ERR and RTY cannot coincide. Payload belongs to a strobed request. This interface has no RTY signal."}]},{"source":"third_party/wb2axip_candidate/README.md","sha256":"e21ffe5300b43bf2c38286ee8f2e201606d0a93f974b67f4c7235bd605904dd3","excerpts":[{"start_line":188,"end_line":196,"text":"188: - [WBXBAR](rtl/wbxbar.v) is a fully function N master to M slave Wishbone
189:   [crossbar](https://zipcpu.com/blog/2019/07/17/crossbar.html).  Unlike my
190:   Unlike my [earlier WB interconnects](https://zipcpu.com/blog/2017/06/22/simple-wb-interconnect.html), this one guarantees that the
191:   ACK responses won't get crossed, and that misbehaving slave accesses will
192:   be timed out.  The core also has options for checking for starvation
193:   (where a master's request is not granted in a particular period of time),
194:   double-buffering all outputs (i.e. with [skid
195:   buffers](https://zipcpu.com/blog/2019/05/22/skidbuffer.html), and forcing idle
196:   channel values to zero in order to reduce power."}]},{"source":"third_party/wb2axip_candidate/rtl/wbxbar.v","sha256":"d3fd1f2d7fb8909c2c2950c666e04c97d704c501c867201aedab5011ee106d2e","excerpts":[{"start_line":7,"end_line":39,"text":"7: // Purpose:	A Configurable wishbone cross-bar interconnect, conforming
8: //		to the WB-B4 pipeline specification, as described on the
9: //	ZipCPU blog.
10: //
11: // Performance:
12: //	Throughput: One transaction per clock
13: //	Latency: One clock to get access to an unused channel, another to
14: //	place the results on the slave bus, and another to return, or a minimum
15: //	of three clocks.
16: //
17: // Usage: To use, you'll need to set NM and NS to the number of masters
18: //	(input ports) and the number of slaves respectively.  You'll then
19: //	want to set the addresses for the slaves in the SLAVE_ADDR array,
20: //	together with the SLAVE_MASK array indicating which SLAVE_ADDRs
21: //	are valid.  Address and data widths should be adjusted at the same
22: //	time.
23: //
24: //	Voila, you are now set up!
25: //
26: //	Now let's fine tune this:
27: //
28: //	LGMAXBURST can be set to control the maximum number of outstanding
29: //	transactions.  An LGMAXBURST of 6 will allow 63 outstanding
30: //	transactions.
31: //
32: //	OPT_TIMEOUT, if set to a non-zero value, is a number of clock periods
33: //	to wait for a slave to respond.  Should the timeout expire and the
34: //	slave not respond, a bus error will be returned and the slave will
35: //	be issued a bus abort signal (CYC will be dropped).
36: //
37: //	OPT_STARVATION_TIMEOUT, if set, applies the OPT_TIMEOUT counter to
38: //	how long a particular master waits for arbitration.  If the master is
39: //	"starved", a bus error will be returned."}]},{"source":"https://zipcpu.com/blog/2019/07/17/crossbar.html","representation":"Prose-only intent paraphrase; no implementation examples, expressions or internal signal names included. Older design article is supplementary, not exact-cycle authority.","requirements":[{"location":"What is a Crossbar?; Returning data, paragraphs on nonexistent addresses and abort","summary":"A crossbar connects masters to slaves without mixing their responses. A request outside every configured address range should return an error. A master may end its cycle to abort outstanding work; completed data must not later be attributed to a new transaction. The article motivates aborting a cycle after an error. Exact buffering, arbitration and timeout edge counts require explicit documentation rather than assumptions."}]}],"parameters":{"NM":2,"NS":2,"AW":8,"DW":32,"SLAVE_ADDR":4096,"SLAVE_MASK":61680,"LGMAXBURST":3,"OPT_TIMEOUT":16,"OPT_STARVATION_TIMEOUT":1,"OPT_DBLBUFFER":1,"OPT_LOWPOWER":1},"interface":{"ports":{"inputs":{"i_reset":1,"i_mcyc":2,"i_mstb":2,"i_mwe":2,"i_maddr":16,"i_mdata":64,"i_msel":8,"i_sstall":2,"i_sack":2,"i_sdata":64,"i_serr":2},"outputs":{"o_mstall":2,"o_mack":2,"o_mdata":64,"o_merr":2,"o_scyc":2,"o_sstb":2,"o_swe":2,"o_saddr":16,"o_sdata":64,"o_ssel":8}},"packing":"Port 0 occupies the least-significant slice. Each address is an 8-bit bus address, each data value 32 bits, each select 4 bits. No implicit byte-address shifting.","address_configuration":"Slave0: (addr & 0xf0)==0x00; slave1: (addr & 0xf0)==0x10. Other addresses match neither slave. This is public parameter decoding, not stimulus guidance.","directions":"i_m* are external master inputs; o_m* are DUT responses. o_s* are DUT requests to external slaves; i_s* are external slave responses. i_clk is the common clock.","rows":"[{cycle:int, pre:{all listed inputs and outputs}, post:{all listed outputs}}]; integers of declared width. None may represent undefined inactive payload, never a valid active control/data value.","sampling":"Inputs change while clock low. Pre sampled at +4ns, rising edge at +5ns, post at +9ns, falling edge at +10ns. One row is one physical cycle. Initial trace has two reset cycles followed by quiescence. No extra hidden clock edges.","boundary":"Checker consumes actual public-pin observations, not model offers or driver queue ledgers. Drivers may queue offers and hold pins through acceptance. Do not trust driver internal accounting as functional truth.","environment_contract":"External masters obey pipelined acceptance, holding pending request payload through backpressure except explicit cycle abort/reset. External slaves respond in acceptance order, may wait, and do not generate unsolicited responses. Slave work is canceled on cycle withdrawal/reset. Record violations separately from DUT faults. Exact future JSON offer fields are irrelevant to the pin-based checker."}},"driver_contract":"One action is one physical clock cycle. Fields are unsigned packed integers; master 0 uses the low slice. rst is synchronous active-high reset. offer bit m queues one request for master m; addr has two 8-bit addresses, data two 32-bit payloads, we two direction bits, sel two 4-bit byte masks. The driver holds queued STB/payload until pre-edge CYC & STB & !STALL acceptance and keeps CYC high while queued or accepted work remains. A sampled ERR discards remaining accepted work for that master, preserves unaccepted offers and inserts one counted low-CYC cycle. Reset discards both kinds of work; offer must be zero when rst=1. The slave environment never stalls; it returns ACK or ERR one cycle after acceptance, gated by downstream CYC. slave_error bit s selects ERR for slave s at that response cycle. Read data is 0xa55a0000 OR the accepted address. Idle actions are real counted cycles. Pending work survives across appended rounds through cumulative replay. No automatic tail drain is added.","field_widths":{"rst":1,"offer":2,"addr":16,"data":64,"we":2,"sel":8,"slave_error":2},"rtl":{"format":"Original source line number: complete active RTL code. Verilator -E with the simulation macros. Comments, whitespace-only lines and inactive preprocessor branches omitted; no active logic omitted. Raw coverage source coordinates remain original.","sources":{"third_party/wb2axip_candidate/rtl/skidbuffer.v":"79:`default_nettype none
81:module skidbuffer #(
83:parameter	[0:0]	OPT_LOWPOWER = 0,
84:parameter	[0:0]	OPT_OUTREG = 1,
86:parameter	[0:0]	OPT_PASSTHROUGH = 0,
87:parameter		DW = 8,
88:parameter	[0:0]	OPT_INITIAL = 1'b1
90:) (
92:input	wire			i_clk, i_reset,
93:input	wire			i_valid,
94:output	wire			o_ready,
95:input	wire	[DW-1:0]	i_data,
96:output	wire			o_valid,
97:input	wire			i_ready,
98:output	reg	[DW-1:0]	o_data
100:);
102:wire	[DW-1:0]	w_data;
104:generate if (OPT_PASSTHROUGH)
105:begin : PASSTHROUGH
107:assign	{ o_valid, o_ready } = { i_valid, i_ready };
109:always @(*)
110:if (!i_valid && OPT_LOWPOWER)
111:o_data = 0;
112:else
113:o_data = i_data;
115:assign	w_data = 0;
118:/*verilator lint_off UNUSED*/
120:wire	unused_passthrough;
121:assign	unused_passthrough = &{ 1'b0, i_clk, i_reset };
123:/*verilator lint_on UNUSED*/
125:end else begin : LOGIC
128:reg			r_valid;
129:reg	[DW-1:0]	r_data;
133:initial if (OPT_INITIAL) r_valid = 0;
134:always @(posedge i_clk)
135:if (i_reset)
136:r_valid <= 0;
137:else if ((i_valid && o_ready) && (o_valid && !i_ready))
139:r_valid <= 1;
140:else if (i_ready)
141:r_valid <= 0;
146:initial if (OPT_INITIAL) r_data = 0;
147:always @(posedge i_clk)
148:if (OPT_LOWPOWER && i_reset)
149:r_data <= 0;
150:else if (OPT_LOWPOWER && (!o_valid || i_ready))
151:r_data <= 0;
152:else if ((!OPT_LOWPOWER || !OPT_OUTREG || i_valid) && o_ready)
153:r_data <= i_data;
155:assign	w_data = r_data;
160:assign o_ready = !r_valid;
166:if (!OPT_OUTREG)
167:begin : NET_OUTPUT
172:assign	o_valid = !i_reset && (i_valid || r_valid);
177:always @(*)
178:if (r_valid)
179:o_data = r_data;
180:else if (!OPT_LOWPOWER || i_valid)
181:o_data = i_data;
182:else
183:o_data = 0;
186:end else begin : REG_OUTPUT
191:reg	ro_valid;
193:initial if (OPT_INITIAL) ro_valid = 0;
194:always @(posedge i_clk)
195:if (i_reset)
196:ro_valid <= 0;
197:else if (!o_valid || i_ready)
198:ro_valid <= (i_valid || r_valid);
200:assign	o_valid = ro_valid;
205:initial if (OPT_INITIAL) o_data = 0;
206:always @(posedge i_clk)
207:if (OPT_LOWPOWER && i_reset)
208:o_data <= 0;
209:else if (!o_valid || i_ready)
210:begin
212:if (r_valid)
213:o_data <= r_data;
214:else if (!OPT_LOWPOWER || i_valid)
215:o_data <= i_data;
216:else
217:o_data <= 0;
218:end
222:end
224:end endgenerate
228:/*verilator coverage_off*/
229:/*verilator lint_off UNUSED*/
230:wire	unused;
231:assign	unused = &{ 1'b0, w_data };
232:/*verilator lint_on UNUSED*/
233:/*verilator coverage_on*/
